+//! Who the peer is, proved rather than claimed.
+//!
+//! `meshkv/5.1-multi-node-topology` let a node *say* its name in one line of text. That is enough to
+//! route a message and nothing else: any process that can open a socket can claim to be `n2`.
+//! `meshkv/5.9-mtls` is the step where the claim becomes a proof — a certificate that a peer
+//! presents, a trust store that decides whether to believe it, and a clock that decides whether it
+//! is still valid.
+//!
+//! Three rules, and each one is a way a cluster is compromised:
+//!
+//! * **the issuer has to be trusted.** A certificate is a statement by somebody; the store is the
+//!   list of somebodies this node believes. A certificate from a stranger is a stranger.
+//! * **the window has to contain now.** Certificates expire — that is the mechanism by which a
+//!   compromised key stops being useful — and a node that ignores the window has a certificate
+//!   store that only grows.
+//! * **the name in the certificate has to be the name that was claimed.** A valid certificate for
+//!   `n2` proves the peer is `n2`; it does not prove the peer is `n3`, and a handshake that checks
+//!   the certificate but not the name has authenticated *somebody* rather than *this peer*.
+//!
+//! The result of verification is an [`Identity`], and that type is the point: it cannot be built by
+//! hand, so any code that takes an `Identity` has a proof in hand rather than a string somebody
+//! typed. This is `domain/parse-dont-validate@apply` in a security setting — the difference between
+//! a check and a guarantee is whether the unchecked value can still reach the code that cares.
+//!
+//! Time is injected, for the reason `5.6` gave: a validity window read from the wall clock cannot be
+//! tested at its boundaries, and the boundaries are the whole subject.
+
